    rdf:value """wizened. He advanced quite alone to Burton, laid one hand on Burton’s arm to prevent any undue familiarity and took a banana He stepped back some paces and ate it, deliberately peeling it, and pulling off the little inside fibres, all the other monkeys watching him. In this way he took 4, eating them one at a time. Then he gave the signal and the other monkeys swooped in, watching, fighting, clamouring.
We found Arnold and Harold nice big, shy boys, who suffered our presence, but I am sure danced a war-dance of joy as soon as we went. Burton’s sister who keeps house for him, is rather awful, so hopelessly “lower middle” in every way.
We got home about 5 and rested and then went to dine with the Robinsons, who
had no other guests. They told """^^xsd:string ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:index 92 ;
